  "name": "Yanomami shamanic chant and reahu feast song",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Vocal practices of the Yanomami of the Brazil–Venezuela borderlands in Roraima and Amazonas. Shamans inhale yãkoana snuff and chant to call down and voice the xapiri spirits, and at the reahu, the intercommunity funerary feast, hosts and guests sing at night in the village plaza and men exchange wayamou, a chanted ceremonial dialogue. Davi Kopenawa described the xapiri and their songs in The Falling Sky, written with Bruce Albert, and ethnographers have recorded shamanic and feast singing on both sides of the border.",
